     which means that if a regular expression matches another defined mount point,
     it will override the previously defined threshold.
 
+    A mount can also be configured with a ``minimum`` and/or ``maximum`` usage
+    threshold, instead of the plain percentage shown above. ``maximum`` behaves
+    exactly like the plain percentage form: it emits a beacon when usage rises
+    to or above the threshold. ``minimum`` emits a beacon when usage falls to
+    or below the threshold, which is useful for catching a mount that normally
+    stays busy but has quietly disappeared (for example, a filesystem that
+    failed to mount, leaving an empty directory on the root filesystem in its
+    place).
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /: 63%
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                minimum: 5%
+                maximum: 50%
+            - /mnt/backup:
+                minimum: 10%
+
